<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Muntafiq confederation</h1><p class="st-meta">1530 CE – 1871 CE (approx.) · capital Suq al-Shuyukh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful Arab tribal confederation of the lower Euphrates, led by the Al Sa'dun sheikhs, that dominated southern Iraq for three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Muntafiq were a confederation of Arab tribes in the marshes and farmlands between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samawah">Samawa</a>. From the 16th century their leadership was held by the Al Shabib, later called Al Sa'dun, a family claiming descent from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sharifate-of-mecca">Sharifs of Mecca</a>. Their power grew as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire's</a> control of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> remained weak.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sheikhs collected tribute from tribal farmers growing dates and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, controlled river traffic and often defied Ottoman governors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, at times taking Basra itself. Their leaders were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a>, while most tribesmen were Shia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuwayni-ibn-abdullah">Thuwayni ibn Abdullah</a> was a dominant figure in the late 18th century, and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suq-al-shuyukh">Suq al-Shuyukh</a> served as their market and base.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the Ottoman reforms of 1871, governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midhat-pasha">Midhat Pasha</a> made the Muntafiq lands a sanjak, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-pasha-al-sadun">Nasir Pasha al-Sa'dun</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasiriyah">Nasiriyah</a> as its capital. The sheikhs became landlords and officials, losing their independent power.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 1530 CE – 1871 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:90.15%;width:6.79%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1530 CE – 1871 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Suq al-Shuyukh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thuwayni ibn Abdullah, Nasir Pasha al-Sa'dun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Muntafiq, Emirate of Muntafiq, Al Sa'dun</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1700&amp;focus=muntafiq-confederation" aria-label="Muntafiq confederation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5785 955 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Muntafiq confederation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6105 1211h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6105 1211h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iraq. <a href="/atlas?year=1700&amp;focus=muntafiq-confederation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

